+# Daily Grind account UI
+
+Static account page with responsive passkey management. No server or build step is needed. The supplied site has no authentication backend, so missing API routes (404 or static HTML fallback) display a labeled preview with example passkeys and browser-local edits. Network failures, authorization failures, and server errors never fall back to a preview.
+
+## Account API integration
+
+Implement authenticated, ownership-checked routes under `/api/account/passkeys`, with appropriate same-origin/CSRF protection. Never accept a client-supplied account ID as authorization.
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`: `{ rpId, passkeys: [{ id, credentialId, name, device, provider, createdAt, lastUsed, current }] }`. `credentialId` is the real base64url WebAuthn ID; `id` identifies the server record. Device is `laptop`, `phone`, or `usb`. `createdAt` is an ISO date and `lastUsed` is a display label. `current` is optional server-provided device context.
+- `PATCH /api/account/passkeys/:id`: accepts `{ name }`, a trimmed name of 1–50 characters; returns a successful status after persisting. The custom label does not change authenticator account/user identity.
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:id`: revoke the credential before returning success. Optionally return `{ rpId, userId, allAcceptedCredentialIds }` from the same server-side mutation. The latter must be the authoritative, full list of remaining base64url credential IDs and the base64url WebAuthn user handle. This enables `signalAllAcceptedCredentials`. Otherwise, return 204; the UI uses `signalUnknownCredential` with the deleted key's credential ID.
+- Return 401/403 for unavailable sessions; enforce a server-side fallback sign-in policy when deleting a final credential.
+
+Browser signaling runs only after confirmed server deletion and never for preview records. Unsupported/rejected signaling does not undo server revocation; users receive manual password-manager cleanup guidance. Success indicates the browser was notified, not that every authenticator has deleted the key. Signal API requires a supported browser and secure context. See the [WebAuthn specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/#sctn-signal-methods).
+
+Run automated checks with `node --test passkey-service.test.mjs` and syntax checks with `node --check account.js`.
